The full suite is green: **256 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS.**

## Summary

I implemented the rule that creating an owner whose normalized telephone is already used by any other owner is rejected with **409 Conflict**.

**Change — `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** (`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):

After normalizing the incoming telephone (existing logic strips non-digits and requires exactly 10 digits, else 400), and before saving, I added a duplicate check against all existing owners, comparing on the *normalized* form of each stored telephone:

```java
for (Owner existing : this.clinicService.findAllOwners()) {
    if (telephone.equals(normalizeTelephone(existing.getTelephone()))) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
    }
}
```

This reuses the existing `normalizeTelephone` helper and the existing service method (`findAllOwners`), so it works uniformly across all three persistence profiles (jpa/jdbc/spring-data-jpa) without touching the repository layer, staying within ordinary Spring conventions.

**Test adjustment.** The pre-existing `OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.createOwnerSuccess` posted telephone `6085551023`, which is exactly the seed owner "George Franklin" (id=1). Under the new rule that POST is now a genuine duplicate → 409, directly contradicting the new spec. Since the test's intent is "a valid create succeeds," I changed only its telephone to `6085550000` (verified absent from the seed data) so it still exercises a successful create. No other test used a POST that collided with seed data (the validation tests fail at 400 before the check; other helpers save via the repository directly).

The new acceptance tests confirm the behavior: `coreRejectsDuplicateTelephone` (duplicate → 409) and `functionalityAllowsDistinctTelephone` (distinct → 2xx) both pass.
